    <h1 class="article-title">What a court order gets from your file transfer service</h1>
    <p class="article-standfirst">Ten services, checked against their own policies, security pages and transparency reports.</p>
    <div class="article-meta">
      <span>July 2026 · updated 10 October 2026</span>
      <span class="meta-dot"></span>
      <span>7 min read</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <article class="article-body">

    <p>This piece looks at what a file transfer service could hand over if a court ordered it to. A production order in England and Wales and a subpoena in the United States work differently, but both can only reach what the service holds.</p>

    <p>Everything below comes from the services' own published documents, checked on 10 October 2026. Where we couldn't confirm something, we say so.</p>

    <h2>Whose key</h2>

    <p>Every service here says your files are encrypted, and they all are. The difference is who holds the key.</p>

    <p>Some services encrypt your file on their own servers, with a key they keep. They can open the file when they need to: to scan it, to show a preview, or because a court tells them to.</p>

    <p>Others encrypt the file on your own device, before it leaves. The key stays with you and the person you send it to. The service stores something it can't read. This is what security people call end-to-end encryption.</p>

    <p>WeTransfer's privacy policy is clear about which kind it is. It says it may share "your information, including User Content" with law enforcement in response to a subpoena, warrant or government request. WeTransfer and Smash also record your IP address and email address, by their own policies.</p>

    <p>None of this is hidden. A service that can open your files can preview them, scan them for malware and recover them if something goes wrong. It can also produce them.</p>

    <h2>What the record shows</h2>

    <p>The clearest public examples come from Proton, which builds end-to-end encrypted email and storage in Switzerland.</p>

    <p>In 2021, French police wanted to identify a climate activist who used Proton Mail. Their request went through Europol to Swiss authorities, who issued a binding order. Proton began logging the IP address used to sign in to the account (the number that identifies an internet connection) and handed it over. The emails stayed unreadable, because Proton doesn't hold the keys.</p>

    <p>In 2024 it happened another way. Spanish police asked for the recovery email on a Proton account: the backup address used to reset a password. Proton gave it. It was an Apple address, and Apple's records led police to the person.</p>

    <p>In both cases the encryption held. What reached the police was the information around the messages: when an account was used, from where, and what other accounts it was linked to. Security people call this metadata.</p>

    <p>Proton publishes the numbers, which most services don't. In 2025 it received 9,301 orders for Proton Mail, contesting 988 of them. Whatever it hands over, it says it has no means to decrypt the emails or files.</p>

    <p>Proton VPN shows the same rule from the other side. In 2025 it received 59 orders and handed over nothing, because it keeps no connection logs. A service can only produce what it keeps.</p>

    <h2>Ten services side by side</h2>

