    <p>Two of these share a name. Magic Wormhole is an open-source tool, first released in 2016, that moves a file directly between two computers using a short spoken code. Nothing is stored, and both people need to be online at once. wormhole.app is a separate website, run by WebTorrent, LLC in California. It works much like Refueler Share: the key travels in the link, files are kept for 24 hours, and the recipient can start downloading before the upload has finished.</p>

    <p>OnionShare runs on your own computer over Tor, which hides both people's IP addresses; the recipient needs Tor Browser. Proton Drive needs an account to send. Tresorit Send needs none and suggests sending the password separately from the link. crypt.fyi is built for passwords, notes and small files rather than large transfers.</p>

    <p>Our own row has gaps. You can't yet run Refueler Share on your own server, though the code is public under the Apache 2.0 licence. Its encryption isn't post-quantum yet. Both are planned for 2027. It also hasn't had an independent security audit of its code; that will follow once the payment and timestamp work is finished.</p>

    <h2>Ownership can change the terms and conditions</h2>

    <p>A privacy policy describes a company as it is today. Companies change hands.</p>

    <p>Bending Spoons bought WeTransfer in July 2024. A year later, WeTransfer's terms gained a licence to use uploaded files for "improving performance of machine learning models that enhance our content moderation process". After public concern, WeTransfer rewrote the clause within days and said it doesn't use customer content that way. Its current list of sub-processors, dated April 2026, includes Bending Spoons and its affiliates, AWS and two AI companies.</p>

    <p>All of this is in WeTransfer's own documents. It is a reminder that the terms you agreed to can be rewritten by whoever owns the service next. That matters less when the service never held the key.</p>

    <h2>What jurisdiction does</h2>

    <p>Switzerland, the Netherlands, France and the UK each set their own bar for when data must be handed over, but those rules decide how an order is made, not what the service holds. WeTransfer and Smash both store files with AWS, a US company, and the US CLOUD Act lets American authorities require US companies to hand over data they control, wherever it is stored. The same law applies to Cloudflare, which Refueler Share runs on. The host isn't what separates these services. The key is.</p>

    <p>Architecture is what protects you; jurisdiction just determines how long the paperwork takes.</p>

    <h2>What a court order gets from Refueler Share</h2>

    <p>This is the full list of what we, or the companies we run on, could be made to produce.</p>

    <ul class="holds-list">
      <li><strong>The encrypted file.</strong> Without the key it is unreadable, to us as well.</li>
      <li><strong>The file's exact size.</strong> We stopped keeping it in our own records in October 2026; it now travels in the link. But the stored, encrypted parts are only slightly larger than the original (16 bytes per 32 MiB part), so their size gives the exact figure away.</li>
      <li><strong>Timing and settings.</strong> When the file was uploaded and when it expires, how many parts it has, which plan it used, and whether delete-after-download or a download window was set.</li>
      <li><strong>A fingerprint of the password, if you set one.</strong> Today this is a plain SHA-256 hash, and the password reaches our server as typed when the recipient unlocks the file. A weak password could be guessed from the hash. The password only gates the download: it is not part of the key, so the file stays encrypted either way. A fix is designed and on our roadmap. The password will be strengthened on your device and become part of the key, and our server will never see it. We'll update this page when it ships.</li>
      <li><strong>A record that each upload pass was used,</strong> so none can be used twice. It doesn't name you.</li>
      <li><strong>IP addresses, briefly.</strong> Our own systems keep a visitor's IP address for about a minute, to slow down abuse.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Cloudflare runs the servers your browser connects to, between you and our code. Those servers see your IP address and the web address you asked for, including the transfer's ID. They never see the key: that sits after the #, and browsers don't send that part. If you'd rather Cloudflare didn't see your IP, use a VPN, ideally one with multi-hop.</p>

    <p>There are no payment records, because paid plans haven't opened yet. When they do, there will be two ways to pay. Paying by card means the card company and our payment processor hold your name and email, linked to your plan. Paying with bitcoin over Lightning goes through our own node, which sees an amount, a payment ID and a time, but not who paid. Once that route is live, upload passes will be bought separately from transfers, so a payment can't be matched to a file.</p>

    <p>What no order can get from us: the key, the real file name, or the contents. All three live after the # in the link, and that part never reaches us.</p>

    <p>If you sent a file yesterday and deleted the link, the encrypted file is still in our storage: the link works until its expiry date, and the stored data is cleared no later than 92 days after upload. It stays unreadable to anyone without the link, including us.</p>

    <h2>What we can't protect yet</h2>

    <p>Each time you open Refueler Share, your browser downloads our code from Cloudflare. If Cloudflare were forced or hacked into sending altered code, that code could copy the keys of people who send files afterwards. Files already sent would stay safe. Every service that encrypts in the browser shares this weakness, including Proton's web apps, wormhole.app and crypt.fyi. We plan to publish a fingerprint of every version of our code, lock the page to it, and offer ways to send that don't load fresh code on each visit.</p>

    <p>No service on this page can protect a device that is already compromised. If someone controls your computer or phone, they can read the file before it is encrypted. End-to-end encryption protects you from the server. It doesn't replace looking after your own device.</p>

    <p>We'll update this page when any of this changes. From January 2027 we'll also publish a transparency report twice a year, including when there is nothing to report.</p>

